{
  "requirement": "Usage MUST record mandatory input counts and sizes by evidence kind, in UTF-8\nbytes and lines, and the same information for additional inputs requested through\n`habeas` or `ripgrep`. A requirements input MUST also record its number of matched declarations.\nUsage MUST distinguish unique inputs from repeated reads, successful from\nfailed reads, and prepared inputs from those actually sent to the model.\nFile-discovery calls MUST count as tool calls without recording directory\nlistings as evidence or source inputs.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge that the sources satisfy `requirements.authoring.usage_inputs`. The implementation, its `{impl}` marking and its tests all hold up.\n\n**Implementation (all obligations covered)**\n- **Per-input kind, bytes, lines, components:** `UsageLog::prepared` in `src/authoring/usage.rs:132-166` pushes one `InputUsage` per input.\n  - It classifies the input as `outline`, `requirements` or `definition`. These are the only three shapes the sources produce (`InputSelector` in `src/inputs.rs`), so the fallback to `definition` is safe.\n  - It records UTF-8 bytes as the sum of `str::len` plus the newline separators, and records lines as the number of text lines.\n  - It records `components` as the length of `matches`, so a requirements input carries its matched-declaration count.\n- **Mandatory vs additional inputs:** `configured_record` in `src/authoring/mod.rs` calls `usage.prepared(\"mandatory\", …)`. `assess` calls `usage.prepared(<tool name>, &delivery.inputs)` for `habeas` and `ripgrep` deliveries. `read` and `search` in `src/authoring/tools.rs` return the delivered inputs, including the additional term definitions.\n- **Unique vs repeated:** `unique` comes from `self.seen.insert(input.to_string())`. A repeat of a mandatory or earlier input is therefore flagged as non-unique.\n- **Successful vs failed reads:** `UsageLog::tool(name, failed)` in `usage.rs:222-235` increments `tool_calls` and `tool_failures`. `assess` calls it with `failed = true` on dispatch errors.\n- **Prepared vs sent:** `begin_request` sets `submitted_in_request`. `end_request` sets `sent_in_request` only when the provider returned usage, i.e. the request completed. Failed or retried requests therefore leave inputs prepared but not sent.\n- **File discovery:** `list_files` and `outline` return `inputs: Vec::new()`. They still go through `usage.tool(...)`, so they count as tool calls but add no evidence or source inputs.\n\n**Marking:** every function that implements part of this requirement carries `{impl}`: `prepared`, `begin_request`, `end_request`, `tool`, `skipped_tool`, `configured_record` and `assess`. I found no unmarked partial implementation. The extras, `origin` and `components = 1` for non-requirements inputs, are harmless and in the spirit of the requirement.\n\n**Tests**\n- `input_metadata_counts_components_and_reads_without_persisting_source` (`usage.rs:363`) covers:\n  - kinds `outline` and `requirements`;\n  - UTF-8 byte count (the `λ` case) and line count;\n  - `components == 2` for a requirements input;\n  - unique vs repeated, and the `origin` field;\n  - the prepared → submitted → sent transitions;\n  - no source or selector text in the stored usage record.\n- `failed_submission_can_be_confirmed_on_a_later_attempt` (`usage.rs:588`) checks that a failed request leaves `sent_in_request` null and a later successful retry sets it.\n- `tool_outcomes_are_distinct_and_unknown_names_do_not_leak_model_text` (`usage.rs:557`) checks that successful, failed and skipped tool calls are counted separately.\n- `throttled_requests_retry_unchanged_and_record_every_attempt` (`mod.rs:605`) checks `submitted_in_request == 0` and `sent_in_request == 8` after eight failed attempts.\n- The end-to-end `ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme_and_keeps_usage_content_free` (`tests/authoring_cli.rs:232`) checks:\n  - a repeated `habeas` read is non-unique and sent in request 1;\n  - a failed `habeas` read is counted in `tool_failures`;\n  - a `list_files` call is counted in `tool_call_count` while `.` never appears in the evidence inputs;\n  - a mandatory `definition` input has non-zero bytes;\n  - no source text appears in the usage record.\n\n**Minor observations (not gaps):** the per-input records carry `kind` rather than pre-aggregated totals by kind, so totals are derived from the list. Real `habeas` and `ripgrep` inputs are not asserted individually as `requirements` kind in the end-to-end test, but they follow the same code path that the unit test covers.",
